    <!-- Descriptions (with cleaned aHTML/JSON formatting; description text and labels are according to v1.0 and thus without language tags) -->
    <description>
        <description_text xml:lang="en">Gold-silver synergism has been well documented in many scientific works dealing with luminescent nanostructures that are exploitable in biomedical and environmental application. Frequently, the ratio of Au : Ag in synthetic mixtures was varied to influence the extent of Au-Ag synergism of the resulting luminescent gold-silver nanoclusters (LGSN). However, in our approach, a new step, maturing under differing conditions using the same Au : Ag ratio (5 : 1), has been investigated systematically for the very first time. As referent systems, monometallic gold nanoclusters (AuNCs) and protein treated by the conditions of synthesis and maturing were prepared and investigated. The selected types of maturing conditions led to distinct changes in fluorescence characteristics and, consequently, Au-Ag synergism extent (evaluated as the ratio of fluorescence quantum yields (FQY) of GSNC vs. AuNCs). The best synergism was obtained for GSNC matured at 37°C for 2.5h. The stability of luminescent signal of these GSNC was tested in the presence of an excess (till 20 mM) of Cu (II) and/or Fe (III) ions (crucial cofactors in living systems). The same metallic ions concentration caused different extents of GSNC luminescence quenching, for which a plausible reasoning is suggested.</description_text>
        <description_type>
            <iri>https://vocabs.ccmm.cz/registry/codelist/DescriptionType/Abstract</iri>
            <label xml:lang="en">Abstract</label>
        </description_type>
    </description>
    <description>
        <description_text xml:lang="en">The data set is related to the characterization of luminescent AuAg nanoclusters being synthesized in the laboratory of Dept. of Experimental Physics, Faculty of Science, Palacký University Olomouc. The laboratory synthesis is followed by varying maturing conditions and subsequently by purification of the nanocomposites. Then, the characterization of gold-silver luminescent nanoclusters embedded in protein-matrix is performed by means of several experimental techniques available such as: UV-vis spectroscopy, fluorescence spectroscopy, circular dischroism, dynamic light scattering, zeta potential measurements, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, high-resolution scanning transmission electron microscopy, energy dispersive spectroscopy. Based on fluorescence quantum yield, synergism of AuAg is assessed. Finally, the impact of selected metal cations (copper and ferric ions) on AuAg nanocomposite luminescence is evaluated.</description_text>
        <description_type>
            <iri>https://vocabs.ccmm.cz/registry/codelist/DescriptionType/Methods</iri>
            <label xml:lang="en">Methods</label>
        </description_type>
    </description>
    <description>
        <description_text xml:lang="en">The sets of experimental data were recorded on several instruments within Palacký University Olomouc. In cases where possible (UV-vis, fluorescence, DLS, zeta potential, XPS, CD), the data are in csv format enabling any replotting.</description_text>
        <description_type>
            <iri>https://vocabs.ccmm.cz/registry/codelist/DescriptionType/TechnicalInfo</iri>
            <label xml:lang="en">Technical Info</label>
        </description_type>
    </description>
